s of 2x2 Systems of Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186116110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d07d2d5c-d3e0-595b-84ff-e18597a3d040" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115743 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449304 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186115743 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449305 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186115743 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449306 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186115743 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32088" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115743 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115743 #edb/ref 1071 "Let \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)=0$\n- **II.** $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$\n- **III.** ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186115743 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "44e70f28-a4b9-56ce-989d-752d9ef0f612" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115743 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the first part of the invertible matrix theorem, the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ must exist.\n\nFinally, only statement II is true." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186115749 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460751 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186115749 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460752 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186115749 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460753 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186115749 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32167" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115749 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115749 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times 2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n- **I.** $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution\n- **II.** $Ax=0$ has infinitely many solutions\n- **III.** $det(A)\\ne 0$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186115749 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e42babae-3f60-5ad6-9c62-eaf91af5f106" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115749 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options I and III give us true statements:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution.* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $det(A)\\ne 0.$* $\\,✓$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186115884 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449394 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186115884 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449395 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186115884 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449396 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186115884 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47653" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115884 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115884 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne -1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)\\ne 0$\n\n- **II.** $Ax=0$ has no solutions\n\n- **III.** The lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2$ intersect at infinitely many points" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186115884 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "07b54935-f6c1-5cc5-a220-23bab58f39a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115884 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =6\\cdot k-2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6k+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $6k+6\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -1.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be a unique zero solution to $Ax=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statement I is true." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186116110 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116110 #edb/ref 1061 16471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116110 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "836cc4ee-5cd5-58a8-8fc6-487e670dc062" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116110 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116111 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116111 #edb/ref 1061 16472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116111 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "33db29bf-42ca-5174-b04b-c5529dc2bc98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116111 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116112 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116112 #edb/ref 1061 16473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116112 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2a71d8e7-c420-59a3-927f-619b1fbcc37e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116112 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116113 #edb/ref 1061 16474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116113 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "72cfa9ea-e123-5f2c-bad3-38a7904ca9f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116113 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116491 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider a system of two linear equations with two variables \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand let\n![](images/73/73c4f1122f53beb4bea2619922226e641d7ac1dd2bb611106d8a33dc13a7abbd.svg)\n\nRecall that we can write the system as $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}.$ The **invertible matrix theorem** says the following statements are equivalent:\n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible (that is, $A^{-1}$ exists).\n\n2\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$ (we call such systems consistent independent).\n\n3\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique solution ![](images/9f/9f196772fa8ca5829a00c8f4378f2a51f423cf6e27fdc8470e245be0c4005f2c.svg)\n\n4\\. The lines ${\\color{red}{a_{11}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{12}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_1}}$ and ${\\color{red}{a_{21}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{22}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_2}}$ intersect at one point.\n\n5\\. The  determinant of $A$ is nonzero (that is, $\\det(A) \\neq 0$)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116491 #edb/ref 1065 3050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116491 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5a0ce34c-f39a-5c24-bab3-840ff974402c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116491 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260154 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has \n the unique solution $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186260154 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260154 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "20c9c114-1eee-549d-a821-19d7a5bb07dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260244 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ is singular" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186260244 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260244 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2a09b0fb-9cb8-54d7-a5f5-b68405cf5da2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261001 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has \n no solution" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186261001 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261001 #edb/ref 