atrix equation $Ax=b$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)\\ne 0$\n\n- **II.** $A$ is  invertible\n\n- **III.** $Ax=0$ has a nonzero solution" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186262851 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cd31265e-20ac-5d02-8e25-ee3bd3fdfd6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262851 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186262856 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449182 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186262856 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449183 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186262856 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449184 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186262856 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127943" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262856 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262856 #edb/ref 1071 "Let \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- **II.** $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$\n- **III.** ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186262856 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "98a38053-dfdc-5995-9d99-1db68e6c6765" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262856 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the first part of the invertible matrix theorem, the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ must exist.\n\nFinally, only statements I and II are true." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186262876 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449185 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186262876 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449186 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186262876 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449187 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186262876 #edb/ref 1069 "q-128124" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262876 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262876 #edb/ref 1071 "Let \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)=0$\n- **II.** $A$ is invertible\n- **III.** $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186262876 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d4f36ed6-3fc6-5f37-801d-3dafa17482c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262876 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statements II and III are true." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186262880 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186464769 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186262880 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186464770 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186262880 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186464771 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186262880 #edb/ref 1069 "q-128152" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262880 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262880 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times 2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n- **I.** $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution\n- **II.** The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n- **III.** $det(A)=0$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186262880 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7075e5f8-1ac7-5f5f-9b8e-5fb44e2f53d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262880 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options I and II give us true statements: \n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution.* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.* $\\,✓$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186262881 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262881 #edb/ref 1069 "q-128154" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262881 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262881 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the lines $-x+y=1$ and $x+ky=1$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262881 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6e29e8bd-10c2-56d6-8b4e-fe5bb28bfb99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262881 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if the lines $-x+y=1$ and $x+ky=1$ intersect at one point, then \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis invertible and $det(A)\\ne 0.$ \nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the lines.\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has \n the unique solution $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186408166 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/checkbox #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186448266 #edb/ref 1080 "false" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448266 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448266 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "31393764-2a3b-5f94-a8ad-ec6c335171fd" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448267 #edb/ref 1080 "true" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448267 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448267 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "553375c2-cf85-5f9d-bbb0-2230855cf965" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448268 #edb/ref 1080 "false" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448268 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448268 #edb/ref 1083 #uui